# Pitwall settings (example). Copy to .env and uncomment only what you want to change.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Pitwall reads .env from the folder you START it in (the release or repository folder when you use
# `uv run pitwall ...`, or the folder the background service runs from). An environment variable with the same name
# wins over .env. When you move to a new release, copy this file into the new release folder.
# Every value below is the built-in default unless the comment says otherwise. Full list: src/pitwall/config.py.
# .env is ignored by git. Never commit API keys.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- basics
# Where the database, raw captures, settings and the access token are stored. A relative path is relative to the
# folder Pitwall starts in. An absolute path (e.g. /Users/<you>/pitwall-data or C:\Users\<you>\pitwall-data) keeps
# your data outside the release folder, so it stays put when you update. The macOS background service
# (scripts/pitwall-service.sh) sets this itself: for it, set PITWALL_DATA_DIR in the shell when you run `install`.
# The Windows scripts (scripts\windows\*.ps1) read it from here. `pitwall simulate` and `make demo` use it too.
# PITWALL_DATA_DIR=data

# Dashboard and API. 0.0.0.0 = reachable from other devices on your network (they need the access token).
# Use 127.0.0.1 to allow this computer only.
# PITWALL_HTTP_HOST=0.0.0.0
# PITWALL_HTTP_PORT=8770

# Token that phones and tablets on your network need. Leave unset: Pitwall generates one and saves it in
# <data dir>/access_token. `uv run pitwall token` prints the link with the token.
# PITWALL_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- telemetry
# Address and port Pitwall listens on for F1 25 telemetry. Set the same port in the game.
# PITWALL_UDP_BIND=0.0.0.0
# PITWALL_F1_UDP_PORT=20777

# Keep raw packets (data/captures/*.pwcap) so sessions can be reprocessed after a fix. A real F1 25 session at
# 60 Hz takes about 400 MB per hour of driving.
# PITWALL_RECORD_RAW=true

# Seconds without packets before a session is closed.
# PITWALL_SESSION_IDLE_TIMEOUT_S=60

# Other games. These are only the starting values: the Connect page saves your choices in <data dir>/settings.json.
# Gran Turismo 7 (Pitwall sends a heartbeat to the console's UDP 33739 and listens on 33740):
# PITWALL_GT7_ENABLED=false
# PITWALL_GT7_CONSOLE_IP=
# PITWALL_GT7_VARIANT=C
# Forza Data Out (Forza Horizon 6 binds ports 5200-5300 itself, so keep this outside that range):
# PITWALL_FORZA_ENABLED=false
# PITWALL_FORZA_UDP_PORT=5301

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ AI engineer (language model)
# Optional. Coaching never uses a model, and the engineer answers from the facts when no model is reachable.
# Set this to false if you have no model server. Settings > AI engineer in the dashboard (Off / Local / Cloud)
# takes over from these values once you save it there. The `pitwall ask` and `pitwall llm-check` commands use
# these values, never the dashboard's; `pitwall ask` honours PITWALL_LLM_ENABLED=false, and --no-llm does the
# same for one call.
# PITWALL_LLM_ENABLED=true

# Any OpenAI-compatible server. The default port 8081 is not a standard one: llama.cpp's llama-server uses 8080,
# LM Studio http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1, Ollama http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1.
# PITWALL_LLM_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8081/v1
# Leave unset to use the first model the server reports.
# PITWALL_LLM_MODEL=
# Key for a local server started with an API key (only ever sent to local addresses).
# PITWALL_LLM_API_KEY=
# PITWALL_LLM_TIMEOUT_S=90
# PITWALL_LLM_MAX_TOKENS=350

# Cloud model keys (PITWALL_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, PITWALL_OPENAI_API_KEY, PITWALL_OPENROUTER_API_KEY,
# PITWALL_GEMINI_API_KEY, PITWALL_GROQ_API_KEY, PITWALL_MISTRAL_API_KEY, PITWALL_CUSTOM_API_KEY) are NOT read from
# this file. Enter them in Settings > AI engineer, or export them in the shell that starts Pitwall.
# See docs/llm-providers.md.

# Keep the engineer conversation history in the database.
# PITWALL_STORE_CONVERSATIONS=true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- coaching
# Starting values for the radio. The driver in the seat and the Radio page can change them while Pitwall runs.
# Coaching intensity: quiet | normal | detailed.
# PITWALL_COACHING_INTENSITY=normal
# Braking calls: off | focus (the 1-3 corners where you lose most time) | markers | all.
# PITWALL_CUE_MODE=focus

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- voice radio
# The radio needs the microphone of the device you talk into; browsers allow it only over HTTPS (or on this
# computer itself). See docs/install.md.
# PITWALL_VOICE_ENABLED=true

# Speech recognition: parakeet (Apple Silicon Macs only) | whispercpp (a whisper.cpp server) | none (typed only).
# On Windows, Linux and Intel Macs there is no Parakeet: spoken questions need whispercpp and a running server
# (tested against a mock server only); typed questions always work.
# PITWALL_STT_ENGINE=parakeet
# PITWALL_WHISPERCPP_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8178

# Engineer voice on the server: kokoro (natural voice, needs scripts/setup-tts.sh; falls back to `say`) |
# say (macOS voices) | sapi (Windows voices) | none (text only; the iPad can still speak with its own voice).
# On Windows, kokoro and say mean sapi.
# PITWALL_TTS_ENGINE=kokoro
# Kokoro voices: bm_fable, bm_george, bf_emma, bf_isabella. With TTS_ENGINE=say, a macOS voice name.
# PITWALL_TTS_VOICE=bm_fable
# PITWALL_TTS_SPEED=1.1
# macOS voice used when the natural voice is not available.
# PITWALL_TTS_FALLBACK_VOICE=Daniel
# Speaking rate for `say`, in words per minute.
# PITWALL_TTS_RATE_WPM=200
# Windows (SAPI): an installed voice, e.g. "Microsoft Hazel Desktop" ("Hazel" is enough; empty = an en-GB voice if
# one is installed, else another English one). `uv run pitwall doctor` lists the installed voices.
# PITWALL_TTS_SAPI_VOICE=
# SAPI speaking rate: -10 (slow) .. 10 (fast), 0 = the voice's default.
# PITWALL_TTS_SAPI_RATE=0

# F1 25 wheel push-to-talk: the "UDP Action" button you assign in the game's controls (udp_action_1 .. 12).
# PITWALL_VOICE_PTT_BUTTON=udp_action_1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- updates
# Settings > System shows when a new Pitwall release is out. auto: while that page is open, it asks GitHub at most once
# a day, never while someone is driving (one HTTPS request to api.github.com, which sees your IP address and the Pitwall version; nothing else is
# sent) | manual: only when you press "Check now" or run `uv run pitwall update --check` | off: Pitwall never
# contacts GitHub on its own. Updating is always your decision: `uv run pitwall update` (docs/install.md "Updating").
# PITWALL_UPDATE_CHECK=auto

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Tailscale
# Requests through `tailscale serve` are signed in by Tailscale identity. The owner of this computer's tailnet
# login is trusted automatically; list other logins here as a JSON list, for example ["someone@example.com"].
# Everyone else needs the access token.
# PITWALL_TAILSCALE_TRUST_OWNER=true
# PITWALL_TAILSCALE_ALLOWED_LOGINS=[]
